<h2 id="who-its-for">🎯 Who Should Use Docparser</h2>

**Strong fit:**

Fine jewelry retailers who receive 50+ supplier PDFs per month and are manually re-entering data into inventory or accounting systems. Multi-location groups dealing with vendor consolidation across different buyer preferences — where each buyer has their own supplier roster and each supplier has its own document format. Estate and consignment dealers who receive irregular memo and consignment agreement PDFs that still need to be logged systematically.

Retailers running QuickBooks Online, NetSuite, or spreadsheet-based inventory who want a structured extraction layer without replacing their existing system of record. Operations teams who have tried to solve this with optical character recognition alone and found the unstructured output unusable at scale.

**Weaker fit:**

Single-location boutiques with fewer than three active suppliers and low monthly PO volume — the setup investment will not return meaningful time savings at that scale. Retailers who need a full procure-to-pay platform with approval workflows, three-way matching, and vendor portals. Those operations should evaluate platforms like Tipalti, Stampli, or Bill.com before arriving at Docparser.

Retailers whose suppliers already offer EDI or direct API integration — if your major vendor pushes structured data directly into your ERP, you do not need a parsing layer for that relationship.

